WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.233
Development of Process Capability Baselines

2
00:00:05.233 --> 00:00:08.233
Welcome back to this session on baselines.

3
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:13.666
Specifically in this segment, we're going to be looking at the idea of a quality baseline.

4
00:00:13.666 --> 00:00:17.666
Now, to begin to understand how we're going to look at quality.

5
00:00:17.666 --> 00:00:21.199
We can measure quality on any number of different dimensions,

6
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.000
but specifically the one we’re going to look at is the idea of sigmas.

7
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.966
How many sigmas is your process?

8
00:00:28.966 --> 00:00:32.899
Okay, now what is a Sigma?

9
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:38.900
A sigma. in a statistical sense, is like a ruler.

10
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:46.000
Now, here comes the question, if I have variation,

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.966
how can I measure variation?

12
00:00:52.966 --> 00:00:58.099
How can I quantify the extent of variation that's present?

13
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.766
Let's say for example, I go out and I produce a bunch of lines.

14
00:01:03.766 --> 00:01:11.332
There's the lines I produce. I can now put a ruler on there and measure those lines.

15
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:17.066
Like, there's about a half-inch. There’s about three quarters of an inch.

16
00:01:17.066 --> 00:01:22.332
There's about one inch. There's approximately two inches.

17
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:27.733
There is approximately 1.25 inches.

18
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:32.966
And there’s about 1.5 inches. Well, that's actually about 1.5 too.

19
00:01:32.966 --> 00:01:38.332
Now as you can see, I have variations in those measurements.

20
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:42.133
So, how much variation is present?

21
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:45.866
Well, for variation we don't have things like an inch,

22
00:01:45.866 --> 00:01:49.866
a pound, miles per hour.

23
00:01:49.866 --> 00:01:56.432
We don't have that kind of standard. What we do have is a concept called

24
00:01:56.433 --> 00:02:02.099
the standard deviation.

25
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.633
For any given set of data we compute the standard deviation.

26
00:02:05.633 --> 00:02:11.399
Just like this, we can take that set of data and compute the standard deviation.

27
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.000
So for that set of data, it's a standard amount,

28
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.466
or the typical deviation that exists here. So that makes it like an inch or a pound.

29
00:02:21.466 --> 00:02:25.799
But only for that set of data. So, it's like a standard.

30
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.700
Now, here's the way the sigmas works. We gotta switch concept here.

31
00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.966
Let's imagine that we are measuring IQ.

32
00:02:34.966 --> 00:02:38.432
Intelligence quotient, how smart somebody is.

33
00:02:38.433 --> 00:02:43.499
So, I go out into the world and I give an exam to a lot of people to measure IQ.

34
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:48.633
So, here's a scale of IQ. And I find that the average is about 100.

35
00:02:48.633 --> 00:02:54.633
Well, I can come over here and count the number of people with an IQ, say between

36
00:02:54.633 --> 00:03:01.033
say between 102 and 98. So, that's 102 and that's 98.

37
00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:07.999
And I can make a bar, that corresponds to the height of the number of people

38
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.866
that I was able to

39
00:03:11.866 --> 00:03:17.632
find with an IQ between 102 and 98.

40
00:03:17.633 --> 00:03:23.699
Then I can go over here and say, between 102 and 106.

41
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.466
And count the number of people.

42
00:03:27.466 --> 00:03:33.299
Once again, that makes a height of a bar.

43
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:39.566
I could come on the other side of that, between, say, 98 and 94.

44
00:03:39.566 --> 00:03:44.399
And count the number of people. See, that would fill in.

45
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:50.766
So if I kept doing that, for those intervals of size four

46
00:03:50.766 --> 00:03:57.666
what I would wind up with is a graph.

47
00:03:57.666 --> 00:04:02.766
That have fewer, and fewer, and fewer people

48
00:04:02.766 --> 00:04:07.999
as you head over here toward Einstein's way. And the same thing would hold true,

49
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.966
as my kids would say, as you head over here in my direction.

50
00:04:12.966 --> 00:04:16.532
Now of course there's no absolute zero point here. We’re just throwing that in.

51
00:04:16.533 --> 00:04:21.333
Well my kids, would of course disagree with that.  But nonetheless we call this a histogram.

52
00:04:21.333 --> 00:04:27.333
Now, if I take and identify

53
00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:33.499
the midpoint of each interval here. And interconnect those

54
00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.833
with a smooth curve,

55
00:04:36.833 --> 00:04:41.566
we’re left with this bell-shaped thing, that looks like a bell.

56
00:04:41.566 --> 00:04:45.532
Now, I'm going to redraw that, because that bell

57
00:04:45.533 --> 00:04:45.566
is what we call the normal distribution.

58
00:04:45.566 --> 00:04:55.532
Things like IQ, our hair thickness, the height of people, the weight of people.

59
00:04:55.533 --> 00:05:02.566
Many things naturally distribute themselves, in this world, as a bell curve.

60
00:05:02.566 --> 00:05:07.799
Or as the normal distribution, as many say.

61
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:12.066
Now what we’re going to do, is we're going to come back and visit this idea

62
00:05:12.066 --> 00:05:16.899
of the normal distribution and how it plays in with the quality baseline.

63
00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:20.633
For right now we're going to just absorb what we just took a look at,

64
00:05:20.633 --> 00:05:25.899
by considering one last graph. And that's a characteristic out in the process.

65
00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:31.000
And you can see that in this case, we have a range between 130 and 70.

66
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.266
Maybe that's the diameter of a shaft.

67
00:05:34.266 --> 00:05:37.066
Or maybe that's the amount of seconds

68
00:05:37.066 --> 00:05:41.232
to respond to a customer in a commercial application.

69
00:05:41.233 --> 00:05:44.999
But here's some actual data and we can see how that

70
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.800
curve would fit to this histogram.

71
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.800
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
